  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe 18Z surface weather map shows the Northern Plains in between \nsystems. The deep 996mb low was across the Mid-Atlantic states, and \nan area of low pressure was set up from offshore British Columbia \nthrough central Saskatchewan. \n\nThe main weather feature impacting our weather will be the low \naround southwestern Canada, which will deepen as it crosses south \ncentral SD lat today into Saturday. The first change is increasing \nhigh clouds nearing from the western Dakotas, with a cloudy sky \nFriday morning. Winds will shift from out of the south to out of the \nwest as a surface trough/cold front pushes from our far western \ncounties at 12Z Friday and slowly slides through the rest of the \nforecast area by the late morning to early afternoon hours. This \nboundary will be accompanied by 1-3 hours of light precipitation in \nthe form of rain. There is the potential for light freezing rain \nacross portions of northeastern SD briefly in the morning. Any \naccumulations of rain or icing from light freezing rain will be very \nlight - with only a 20-40% chance of precipitation.\n\nEven with the weak cold front, warm enough air will allow \ntemperatures to increase into the low 50s Friday, except stay in the \nlow to mid 40s east of the James River. These temperatures are 5 to \nnear 15 degrees above normal for this time of year. Winds will be on \nthe increase, with westerly gusts of 15 to 35 mph in the afternoon \nover the area while weakest across west central MN. The ongoing \nmainly dry weather and strong winds will result in high grassland \nfire danger index values Friday afternoon over portions of central \nSD. Relative humidity values will remain over 45%.\n\nWhile the surface low exits across Ontario Saturday night and \nSunday, gusty winds will remain in place over the Northern Plains. \nExpect wind gusts out of the northwest 20 to 40 mph, once again \nstrongest over portions of central SD. High grassland fire danger \nindex values are again expected over central SD. The potential for \n45+mph wind gusts Saturday have diminished, with only a 35% chance \nover western and Corson and Dewey Counties. There is a 30% chance of \nlight rain, mainly Saturday afternoon, over south central SD \n(Jones/Lyman Counties).\n\nPast Saturday we're in a steady pattern with an elongated lee trough \nset up to our west that looks to move into the Plains States late \nMonday into Tuesday of next week. While mainly dry weather is \nforecast, we can't completely rule out a light winterly mix on \nWednesday.",
